  "question_text": "Suppose $\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\,dx = 2$. Determine the value of:",
  "has_diagram": false,
  "subparts": [
    {
      "subpart": "a",
      "stem": "$\\int_{2}^{0} f(x)\\,dx$",
      "final_answer": "$-2$",
      "mark_breakdown": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Using the reversal of limits property $\\int_b^a f(x)\\,dx = -\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx$"
        },
        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Obtaining $-2$"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$\\int_2^0 f(x)\\,dx = -\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = -2$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Reversing limits of integration changes the sign of the integral.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Recall that $\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx = -\\int_b^a f(x)\\,dx$.",
          "Given $\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 2$, swapping limits yields $-2$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Forgetting that reversing integration limits negates the value."
      }
    },
    {
      "subpart": "b",
      "stem": "$\\int_{0}^{2} (3+2f(x))\\,dx$",
      "final_answer": "$10$",
      "mark_breakdown": [
        {
          "code": "M1",
          "desc": "Splitting the integral using linearity properties"
        },
        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Evaluating $\\int_0^2 3\\,dx = 6$ and $2\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 4$"
        },
        {
          "code": "A1",
          "desc": "Summing to get $10$"
        }
      ],
      "solution": "$\\int_0^2 (3 + 2f(x))\\,dx = \\int_0^2 3\\,dx + \\int_0^2 2f(x)\\,dx = 3(2-0) + 2\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 6 + 2(2) = 6 + 4 = 10$",
      "ai_explanation": {
        "concept_rule": "Linearity property of integrals: $\\int (af(x) + bg(x))\\,dx = a\\int f(x)\\,dx + b\\int g(x)\\,dx$.",
        "step_by_step": [
          "Separate into $\\int_0^2 3\\,dx$ and $2\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx$.",
          "Compute the constant integral: $3 \\times (2 - 0) = 6$.",
          "Use the given value $\\int_0^2 f(x)\\,dx = 2$ to compute $2(2) = 4$.",
          "Add the results: $6 + 4 = 10$."
        ],
        "common_pitfalls": "Forgetting to integrate the constant term $3$ with respect to $x$ over the interval $[0, 2]$."
      }
    },
    {
      "subpart": "c",
      "stem": "$k$ such that $\\int_{0}^{2} (kf(x)+2)\\,dx = 10$.",
      "final_answer": "$3$",
